# The licence units/conversions.sqlc takes when it reads the conversion graph off one quantity.
# Two rows, because a slice stands in for the whole relation on two conditions and not one.
#
# A pin is the whole relation on two conditions
#   Pinning one value of a column and dropping the column answers for the whole relation exactly
#   when the payload is CONSTANT on the key, so there is one answer to give, and the pinned value
#   REACHES every key, so no key is dropped on the way. Either one alone passes while the other
#   fails. `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `pin_is_the_whole_relation`, proves the pair and gives a
#   counterexample to each half. The two subjects here are those two conditions, measured over the
#   two relations they live in: layers/quantities.sqlc for the unit, composition/part_quantities.sqlc
#   for the reach.
#
# A unit belongs to a claim, not to a layer
#   `pm.claim.unit` is filed per position, so nothing in the schema stops a layer from filing its
#   demand in one unit and its nameplate in another. arithmetic/remainder.sqlc already carries a
#   `not comparable` verdict for exactly that layer, so the model not only permits it, it has a
#   word for it. This corpus does not do it, and units/conversions.sqlc pins
#   `quantity = 'nameplate'` on the strength of that.
#
# Which makes the pin a claim about the corpus, and this is where the claim is made
#   The pin's own header stated the query and said to run it. Nothing ran it. units/conversions.sqlc
#   feeds checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close, so the day a layer files two units that rule and
#   the whole unit graph read one quantity of five and say nothing about the other four, and they
#   say it in the shape of a clean answer.
#
# A functional dependency, which is why it is `|π(layer, unit)| = |π(layer)|`
#   The claim is that `(filing, layer) → unit` is a function on layers/quantities.sqlc. Counting
#   the distinct units per layer is the same statement and is what the SQL does, because the count
#   names the offending layers on its way past and an equality of two projections does not.
#
# An absent unit is not a disagreement
#   A quantity filed with no unit makes no claim about units, and `count(DISTINCT unit)` passes
#   over a NULL, so such a row is counted out rather than counted against. That is the reading the
#   pin needs: it converts between two units that were both stated.
#
# The second subject: a part the pin never sees is an edge the unit graph does not have
#   units/conversions.sqlc draws one edge per part with a STATED factor, read at the nameplate. A
#   part whose layers file a demand and no nameplate is a legitimate filing and contributes no
#   edge, so checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close would walk a graph missing an arrow and find no
#   cycle for the best of reasons. Nothing said so until this row: it counts the parts with a
#   stated factor that never reach the pinned quantity.
#
# Its non-vacuity is a column, not a comment
#   Every layer agreeing trivially is what an empty corpus, a corpus that states no units, and a
#   working corpus all look like from the outside. The layers that file a quantity and the layers
#   that state a unit are both inside `holds`, and so are the parts and the stated factors, so a
#   population that stopped arriving fails here rather than passing quietly.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `layer_units`, filing the nameplate in a unit of its own.
-- layers/quantities.sqlc folded to one row per layer: how many units its quantities name.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'layers/quantities' AS subject,
           count(*) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.units > 0) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.units > 1) = 0                               AS holds,
           format('%s layer(s) file a quantity, %s state a unit, %s state more than one%s',
                  count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.units > 0),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.units > 1),
                  coalesce(': ' || string_agg(x.filing || '/' || x.layer, ', '
                                              ORDER BY x.filing, x.layer)
                                   FILTER (WHERE x.units > 1), ''))                  AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT q.filing, q.layer, count(DISTINCT q.unit) AS units
        FROM      (
            :compose(layers/quantities.sqlc)
        ) q
        GROUP BY q.filing, q.layer
    ) x
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'composition/part_quantities',
           count(*) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE y.stated) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE y.stated AND NOT y.at_the_pin) = 0            AS holds,
           format('%s part(s), %s with a stated factor, %s of those file no nameplate%s',
                  count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE y.stated),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE y.stated AND NOT y.at_the_pin),
                  coalesce(': ' || string_agg(y.composition || '/' || y.part_layer, ', '
                                              ORDER BY y.composition, y.part_layer)
                                   FILTER (WHERE y.stated AND NOT y.at_the_pin), ''))
    FROM (
        SELECT r.composition, r.composed_layer, r.part_filing, r.part_layer,
               bool_or(r.factor_state = 'stated')  AS stated,
               bool_or(r.quantity = 'nameplate')   AS at_the_pin
        FROM      (
            :compose(composition/part_quantities.sqlc)
        ) r
        GROUP BY r.composition, r.composed_layer, r.part_filing, r.part_layer
    ) y
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'layer_units'
